.lua
book.chapter(41, "Testing, parity, and performance engineering")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Different tests establish different claims"
.pp
A successful example establishes one observable result. A regression test
guards a known failure mode. A property test checks a relationship across
many inputs. A parity test compares host implementations. A packaging test
checks installed artifacts rather than source-tree assumptions. These claims
overlap, but none substitutes for every other kind of evidence. Choose tests
according to the contract changed rather than simply maximizing the number
of assertions in a file.
.pp
For language work, distinguish acceptance, inferred types, AST structure,
evaluation, output, and diagnostics. Two dialects can print the same final
number while disagreeing about an intermediate AST or an error path. A
parser change may need both successful and rejected inputs. A native handle
change may need ownership and same-session recovery tests. Make the intended
claim visible in the test's name and observations.
.sh 2 "Dialect parity is observable equivalence"
.pp
The repository's parity checker runs shared MetaML sources through C++ and
D executables and compares observable behavior, including AST and trace
modes where requested. Native layouts and allocation strategies may differ.
Primitive names, type signatures, results, ordering, and failure codes must
remain compatible at the language boundary. A new operation implemented in
only one dialect should not be advertised as part of the portable surface.
.lua
book.code("sh", [=[python3 scripts/run_tests.py --cpp build/metaml-repl-cpp --d build/metaml-repl-d
python3 scripts/parity_check.py --cpp build/metaml-repl-cpp --d build/metaml-repl-d --trace
python3 metaml-book/scripts/check_book.py --metaml build/metaml-repl-cpp \
  --pplua metaml-book/build/luaroff/pplua]=], "Language, parity, and book checks use explicit executables")
.endlua
.pp
A missing D compiler means D checks are unavailable, not that parity has
been proved. Report exactly which checks ran. When diagnosing a divergence,
minimize the source while preserving the difference, then compare stages
to find where behavior separates. A small counterexample is more useful
than a large application that only reports different final output without
revealing whether the cause is lexing, inference, or a host primitive.
.sh 2 "Properties outlive implementation details"
.pp
Round-trip serialization, rewrite semantic preservation, stable sorting,
and traversal order are strong candidates for property-style tests. For
bounded integers, formulate arithmetic properties using the language's
actual wrapping or checked contract. For floating values, account for NaN,
infinity, and approximation. A mathematical identity that ignores the
representation can incorrectly label a correct implementation as broken.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[samples := [[], [1], [1, 2, 3], [3, 1, 3, 2]]
Test.check "reverse involution"
  (List.all (fn xs -> List.reverse (List.reverse xs) == xs) samples)
Test.check "sort idempotence"
  (List.all (fn xs -> List.sort (List.sort xs) == List.sort xs) samples)
Test.check "map preserves length"
  (List.all (fn xs -> List.length (List.map (+ 1) xs) == List.length xs) samples)]=],
    "Small deterministic property corpora")
.endlua
.pp
The finite corpus does not prove the properties universally, but it makes
their intended meaning executable and supplies useful regression witnesses.
Generated tests can expand coverage with a fixed seed for reproducibility.
Keep generated programs within a chosen resource budget so a failing test
does not merely hang the suite. Shrinking a failure to a minimal example
often teaches more than increasing the number of random cases.
.sh 2 "Measure the actual representation costs"
.pp
List indexing is linear, repeated append can be quadratic, and a pipeline
can allocate intermediate collections. Tree recursion can consume stack,
while shared graph unfolding can expand dramatically. Tail-call optimization
addresses a particular stack-growth pattern, not every allocation in the
program. Benchmark representative input sizes and shapes, including empty,
skewed, and deeply nested cases, before changing a clear implementation
into a more complicated one.
.pp
Separate startup, library loading, parsing, checking, and evaluation when
measuring. A benchmark that creates a new session for every tiny arithmetic
operation mostly measures setup. A long-lived benchmark can accidentally
benefit from import caches or retained bindings. State the lifecycle used
and include warm and cold measurements when both matter to the deployed
application. Keep output disabled or controlled if printing would dominate.
.sh 2 "Determinism simplifies diagnosis"
.pp
Stable key order, deterministic graph traversal, and normalized rendering
make test failures easier to compare. Normalize externally unordered inputs
before relying on reproducible output. Avoid embedding timestamps, pointer
addresses, or uncontrolled random seeds in golden artifacts. For numerical
libraries, distinguish same-host dialect agreement from cross-platform
bitwise identity; native mathematical libraries can differ at the final bit.
.lua
book.algorithm("Investigate a performance regression", {
    "Reproduce with a fixed source, library revision, and input corpus.",
    "Separate setup, frontend, evaluation, and output costs.",
    "Measure scaling over several input sizes.",
    "Identify repeated traversal, allocation, or conversion at a boundary.",
    "Change the responsible algorithm and preserve semantic tests.",
    "Remeasure the same workload and report both gains and remaining costs."
})
.endlua
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Preserve benchmark inputs alongside results. A reported speedup without the
source, library revision, lifecycle, and input shape is difficult to reproduce
and may measure a different task. Correctness fixtures should continue to
run independently of timing so a faster wrong answer cannot pass as an
engineering improvement.
.pp
Read scripts/run_tests.py, scripts/parity_check.py, tests, and the book's
checker. Add a regression for a malformed placeholder and a successful
same-session evaluation afterwards. Compare repeated nth traversal with a
single fold on increasing list sizes. Explain what each test or measurement
establishes, and avoid reporting skipped dialect checks or unmeasured
complexity improvements as completed evidence.
